1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,740
An evaluation report from three weeks ago
says its numbers were produced "at commit

2
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:14,447
2e76f67". Today the numbers are disputed,
and your CTO asks two questions.

3
00:00:14,787 --> 00:00:19,164
What does that ID pin down: one file,
one change, or the whole project?

4
00:00:19,504 --> 00:00:24,578
And could anything behind that ID have
been altered since then, by accident or on

5
00:00:24,618 --> 00:00:25,083
purpose?

6
00:00:27,423 --> 00:00:32,310
No command answers those. A model does:
what Git stores, and how it names what it

7
00:00:32,350 --> 00:00:35,472
stores. With the model, each
answer is one sentence.

8
00:00:35,512 --> 00:00:40,459
The ID pins down every byte of every tracked
file, the complete history before it,

9
00:00:40,499 --> 00:00:45,142
and who recorded it when. And nothing
behind the ID can change without the ID

10
00:00:45,182 --> 00:00:49,765
changing. This video is where you earn
the right to say those two sentences.

11
00:00:51,605 --> 00:00:56,305
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep
Mastery. Pull up a chair: this is Part 1.

12
00:00:56,345 --> 00:01:01,477
In Part 0 you watched files appear in the
objects folder, inside the dot git folder,

13
00:01:01,517 --> 00:01:04,309
and you didn't open them.
Today you open them.

14
00:01:04,649 --> 00:01:09,916
Two ideas carry this video. The first: a
commit is a snapshot of the whole project,

15
00:01:09,956 --> 00:01:13,816
not a record of what changed.
Hold on to that word, snapshot.

16
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:18,803
In a few minutes it explains why a second
commit didn't store README.md again.

17
00:01:18,843 --> 00:01:23,854
The second: every object is named by a
hash of its own content, which is called

18
00:01:23,894 --> 00:01:29,033
content addressing. Together they also
explain why the IDs on my screen equal the

19
00:01:29,073 --> 00:01:34,340
IDs in your book, and why "Git stores diffs"
is false as a statement about storage.

20
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:39,349
I'll say this once for the whole part,
because you'll want to compare: the commit

21
00:01:39,389 --> 00:01:44,117
IDs on screen are read from replays with
the fixed clock, so they equal the IDs in

22
00:01:44,157 --> 00:01:44,873
the textbook.

23
00:01:46,713 --> 00:01:48,291
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:48,631 --> 00:01:50,043
After this video you can:

25
00:01:50,383 --> 00:01:54,455
Open the first commit of the guided
repository and name the object behind the

26
00:01:54,495 --> 00:01:56,111
commit, its tree and its files.

27
00:01:56,451 --> 00:02:00,728
Show with object IDs that two commits
share the blob of an unchanged file.

28
00:02:01,068 --> 00:02:04,641
Explain why a diff is computed
on demand and is stored nowhere.

29
00:02:04,981 --> 00:02:09,294
Predict whether the same content gets
the same object ID in two unrelated

30
00:02:09,334 --> 00:02:10,845
repositories, and say why.

31
00:02:12,685 --> 00:02:17,439
Start from what you have. Back in video
3, five files appeared in that objects

32
00:02:17,479 --> 00:02:22,541
folder when you made the first commit of
rag-eval. Each file is one object, and two

33
00:02:22,581 --> 00:02:26,537
commands open them. git cat-file
-t prints the type of an object.

34
00:02:26,577 --> 00:02:29,733
git cat-file -p prints its
content in readable form.

35
00:02:29,773 --> 00:02:32,008
Both are 🟢 SAFE: they change nothing.

36
00:02:32,348 --> 00:02:37,392
When you open those five, you find three
kinds. A commit, which contains no file

37
00:02:37,432 --> 00:02:42,476
name and no file content: one tree line,
the author and the committer with their

38
00:02:42,516 --> 00:02:47,624
times, and the message. A tree, which is
one directory at that moment: each entry

39
00:02:47,664 --> 00:02:50,992
has a mode, the type and ID
of an object, and a name.

40
00:02:51,032 --> 00:02:54,996
And a blob, an object that holds
file content and nothing else.

41
00:02:55,036 --> 00:02:56,077
No name, no date.

42
00:02:56,417 --> 00:03:00,185
Git's manual counts four kinds
of data in a repository.

43
00:03:00,225 --> 00:03:05,516
Objects: immutable commits, trees, blobs
and tag objects, named by their IDs.

44
00:03:05,556 --> 00:03:11,055
References, or refs: names for object IDs.
The index: the next commit, as a flat

45
00:03:11,095 --> 00:03:16,248
list of paths. And reflogs: a local
journal of the values each ref has had.

46
00:03:16,288 --> 00:03:20,680
The working tree is a fifth thing,
and it isn't repository data.

47
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:26,376
Snapshots, not diffs. In one sentence: a
commit records the complete state of every

48
00:03:26,416 --> 00:03:31,708
tracked file at one moment, as the ID of
one tree. It doesn't record what changed.

49
00:03:32,048 --> 00:03:36,479
Now precisely. The official data model
lists what a commit must contain: the

50
00:03:36,519 --> 00:03:41,068
directory structure and file contents of
that version, stored as the ID of the

51
00:03:41,108 --> 00:03:45,952
top-level tree. The IDs of its parent
commits. An author and a committer, each with

52
00:03:45,992 --> 00:03:50,835
a time. And a message. It states that Git
stores no diff for a commit, and that git

53
00:03:50,875 --> 00:03:53,718
show calculates one from
the parent when you ask.

54
00:03:54,058 --> 00:03:59,265
So how can full snapshots be cheap? Because
a file whose content didn't change isn't

55
00:03:59,305 --> 00:04:03,763
stored again. The new tree lists the
ID of the blob that already exists.

56
00:04:04,103 --> 00:04:09,333
Content addressing. In one sentence: an
object's ID is a hash of the object's type

57
00:04:09,373 --> 00:04:14,668
and content. So identical content has the
identical ID in every repository on every

58
00:04:14,708 --> 00:04:19,745
machine, and different content has, for
all practical purposes, a different ID.

59
00:04:20,085 --> 00:04:24,748
Precisely. The manual defines the ID,
also called the object name, as a

60
00:04:24,788 --> 00:04:28,258
cryptographic hash of the
object's type and contents.

61
00:04:28,298 --> 00:04:33,689
With the default hash, SHA-1, an ID has 40
hexadecimal digits, and commands accept

62
00:04:33,729 --> 00:04:35,411
and print unique prefixes.

63
00:04:35,751 --> 00:04:39,165
Here's the key: what is hashed
decides what an ID identifies.

64
00:04:39,505 --> 00:04:44,904
For a blob, the bytes of the file are
hashed. Not its name, not its mode, not any

65
00:04:44,944 --> 00:04:49,806
time. So a blob ID identifies one exact
file content, wherever it occurs.

66
00:04:49,846 --> 00:04:54,171
For a tree: for each entry, the
mode, the name and the object ID.

67
00:04:54,511 --> 00:04:59,807
So a tree ID identifies one exact directory
state, including everything below it.

68
00:04:59,847 --> 00:05:05,144
For a commit: the tree ID, the parent IDs,
the author, the committer, both times,

69
00:05:05,184 --> 00:05:10,349
and the message. So a commit ID identifies
one project state, the whole history

70
00:05:10,389 --> 00:05:12,720
behind it, and who recorded it when.

71
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:17,971
Four properties follow. Deduplication:
equal content is stored once, whatever its

72
00:05:18,011 --> 00:05:22,738
path, commit or branch. Immutability: an
object can't be edited, because other

73
00:05:22,778 --> 00:05:27,505
content is another object with another
ID. So "changing a commit" always means

74
00:05:27,545 --> 00:05:32,517
creating a new one. Integrity: Git can
recompute the hash of whatever it reads, so

75
00:05:32,557 --> 00:05:36,978
corruption and tampering are detectable.
And cheap comparison: two files,

76
00:05:37,018 --> 00:05:42,112
directories or project states are equal when
their IDs are equal, and merge and diff

77
00:05:42,152 --> 00:05:44,067
skip everything whose IDs match.

78
00:05:44,407 --> 00:05:49,037
If that felt fast, here it is in one sentence:
the name of a thing is computed from

79
00:05:49,077 --> 00:05:49,599
the thing.

80
00:05:49,939 --> 00:05:53,616
Where this model stops. The
model says "full snapshots".

81
00:05:53,656 --> 00:05:58,927
Later, Git may pack objects into packfiles
and store some of them as differences

82
00:05:58,967 --> 00:06:04,502
against similar objects. That's compression
below the model: git cat-file -p returns

83
00:06:04,542 --> 00:06:08,419
the full content either way.
And there's a real weak point.

84
00:06:08,459 --> 00:06:12,800
Every version of a 2 gigabyte model
file is a new 2 gigabyte blob.

85
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:15,587
That's the subject of the Git LFS chapter.

86
00:06:17,427 --> 00:06:22,305
Two pictures now. The textbook's analogy
for snapshots: a photograph of the whole

87
00:06:22,345 --> 00:06:27,162
whiteboard after every meeting, instead
of a list of what was wiped and written.

88
00:06:27,202 --> 00:06:31,290
Each photograph can be read on its
own, and any two can be compared.

89
00:06:31,330 --> 00:06:36,268
It breaks in one place: an album stores
every photograph in full, while Git stores

90
00:06:36,308 --> 00:06:41,185
each distinct file content once and lets
every snapshot that contains it refer to

91
00:06:41,225 --> 00:06:41,914
that object.

92
00:06:42,254 --> 00:06:46,933
The analogy for content addressing: a
warehouse that shelves each parcel at an

93
00:06:46,973 --> 00:06:51,954
address computed from its content. Two
warehouses in different cities put identical

94
00:06:51,994 --> 00:06:56,855
parcels at identical addresses without
ever talking to each other, and an altered

95
00:06:56,895 --> 00:07:01,876
parcel no longer belongs at its address.
It breaks because nobody finds a parcel by

96
00:07:01,916 --> 00:07:06,898
guessing its content. You need a catalogue
from names to addresses, and in Git that

97
00:07:06,938 --> 00:07:09,076
catalogue is the trees and the refs.

98
00:07:09,416 --> 00:07:13,679
Put the two together and you have the
sentence to keep: a commit is a photograph

99
00:07:13,719 --> 00:07:16,100
whose address is computed
from what is in it.

100
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:20,264
Let's put real IDs on that photograph.

101
00:07:20,604 --> 00:07:26,617
Two commits. The arrow between them is the
parent link, and it points from the newer

102
00:07:26,657 --> 00:07:30,868
commit back to the older one.
Each commit has its own tree.

103
00:07:30,908 --> 00:07:36,272
Now read the rows. config.yaml has a
different ID on the right: a new blob.

104
00:07:36,312 --> 00:07:40,883
judge_prompt.txt and metrics.py
have the same IDs on both sides.

105
00:07:40,923 --> 00:07:44,846
Not equal copies. The same
object, listed by two trees.

106
00:07:45,186 --> 00:07:50,036
Each ID is computed from the IDs below
it, so one changed byte changes every ID

107
00:07:50,076 --> 00:07:55,174
above it. Feed the function two inputs that
differ in one byte, and the two outputs

108
00:07:55,214 --> 00:07:59,384
have nothing in common. You'll see
that in the terminal in a moment.

109
00:08:01,224 --> 00:08:05,765
We start where video 3 stopped: the
first commit of rag-eval, f7c044e.

110
00:08:05,805 --> 00:08:09,365
Predict: does the commit
object contain the file names?

111
00:08:14,205 --> 00:08:19,766
The commit f7c044e has no file name in it:
a tree line, author, committer, message.

112
00:08:19,806 --> 00:08:25,232
Now the third command: the commit ID, a
caret, and the word tree in curly braces.

113
00:08:25,272 --> 00:08:30,900
That notation means "the tree of this commit",
and it prints the top directory, tree

114
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:35,287
e621cb0. In it, README.md is a
blob, and configs is another tree.

115
00:08:35,627 --> 00:08:40,893
A commit, a colon, then a path means "the
object at this path in this commit".

116
00:08:40,933 --> 00:08:45,927
So the tree 0d722fc is the configs
directory, with one blob for eval.yaml.

117
00:08:45,967 --> 00:08:50,416
And the blob 422e9c0 is the content
of that file and nothing else.

118
00:08:50,456 --> 00:08:54,022
Five objects, connected only
by the IDs they contain.

119
00:08:54,362 --> 00:08:58,682
A scorer with three files is committed.
git ls-tree prints the tree of a commit.

120
00:08:59,022 --> 00:09:04,664
Three files, three blob IDs. Now one file
changes, config.yaml, and it's committed

121
00:09:04,704 --> 00:09:10,347
again. git commit -a first stages every
tracked file that was modified or deleted.

122
00:09:10,387 --> 00:09:15,891
Predict, and remember the word snapshot:
the second commit is a full snapshot of

123
00:09:15,931 --> 00:09:19,772
three files. How many of the
three blob IDs will be new?

124
00:09:19,812 --> 00:09:21,643
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

125
00:09:26,483 --> 00:09:33,122
Compare the two listings. judge_prompt.txt
and metrics.py have the same blob IDs in

126
00:09:33,162 --> 00:09:39,721
both commits, f5970a3 and 652e0e2. Only
config.yaml has a new one, f72ae75 instead

127
00:09:39,761 --> 00:09:45,515
of e09e51f. The second snapshot is
complete, and it needed one new blob.

128
00:09:45,855 --> 00:09:46,828
Count everything.

129
00:09:47,168 --> 00:09:52,739
git cat-file --batch-all-objects --batch-check
lists every object with its type and

130
00:09:52,779 --> 00:09:58,284
its size in bytes. Two commits, two trees
and four blobs, where six blobs would be

131
00:09:58,324 --> 00:10:00,312
needed if nothing were shared.

132
00:10:00,652 --> 00:10:05,945
That also answers the question video 3 left
open. Here the second commit added three

133
00:10:05,985 --> 00:10:10,198
object files: the new blob, the new
top-level tree, and the commit.

134
00:10:10,238 --> 00:10:15,023
In Chapter 1 the second commit added
four, because the changed file was in a

135
00:10:15,063 --> 00:10:19,975
subdirectory, so the tree of the configs
directory and the top-level tree both

136
00:10:20,015 --> 00:10:24,483
changed. README.md wasn't stored
again. That's your snapshot, paid off.

137
00:10:24,823 --> 00:10:29,742
Now look for the diff. If Git stored diffs,
the second commit is where one would be

138
00:10:29,782 --> 00:10:30,160
hiding.

139
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:34,339
A tree, a parent, two identities
with times, and a message.

140
00:10:34,379 --> 00:10:36,837
No diff. So where does this come from?

141
00:10:37,177 --> 00:10:42,103
The diff that git show prints is
computed when you ask for it.

142
00:10:42,143 --> 00:10:47,550
Look at the line that starts with
index: e09e51f, two dots, f72ae75.

143
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:54,279
It names the two blobs that Git compared.
Those are the two config.yaml IDs from the

144
00:10:54,319 --> 00:10:55,401
tree listings.

145
00:10:55,741 --> 00:10:59,973
git hash-object computes the ID that
a file's content has as a blob.

146
00:11:00,013 --> 00:11:05,061
First, outside any repository. Predict:
the same line hashed twice, then with one

147
00:11:05,101 --> 00:11:06,192
character changed.

148
00:11:06,532 --> 00:11:13,105
The same bytes give the same ID twice,
b5fee68. One changed character, 0.2 to 0.3,

149
00:11:13,145 --> 00:11:19,879
gives an unrelated ID. And no repository
exists here, so the ID can't depend on one.

150
00:11:20,219 --> 00:11:25,311
Next: two repositories, and two different
file names with the same bytes.

151
00:11:25,351 --> 00:11:30,934
With -w, git hash-object also stores the
object. That form is 🟢 SAFE, because it

152
00:11:30,974 --> 00:11:35,996
only adds an object. Remember the two
warehouses. Same ID, or different?

153
00:11:38,336 --> 00:11:43,527
The same ID, in both. Both repositories
hold an object file at the same path: the

154
00:11:43,567 --> 00:11:47,855
first two digits of the ID name a
directory, the other 38 the file.

155
00:11:47,895 --> 00:11:50,180
Neither file name is recorded in it.

156
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:56,037
So where do names go? Into trees. git
write-tree, also 🟢 SAFE, writes the index as a

157
00:11:56,077 --> 00:11:57,558
tree and prints its ID.

158
00:11:57,898 --> 00:12:03,368
With different file names, the two trees
differ. Then the script renames the file in

159
00:12:03,408 --> 00:12:07,762
server, so that both repositories
contain the same directory state.

160
00:12:07,802 --> 00:12:12,551
git add -A stages the removal of the
old name together with the new file.

161
00:12:12,591 --> 00:12:17,995
And now the tree IDs are equal: 097da7e.
Two repositories that have never exchanged

162
00:12:18,035 --> 00:12:21,012
a byte agree on the name
of a directory state.

163
00:12:22,852 --> 00:12:24,500
Five mistakes to watch for.

164
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:29,371
Saying "Git stores diffs". Root cause:
the output of git show was taken for the

165
00:12:29,411 --> 00:12:34,115
content of the commit; the commit holds a
tree ID, and the diff is calculated from

166
00:12:34,155 --> 00:12:35,388
the parent on request.

167
00:12:35,728 --> 00:12:38,907
Believing a full snapshot per
commit must be expensive.

168
00:12:38,947 --> 00:12:43,588
Root cause: forgetting deduplication; a
file whose content did not change is the

169
00:12:43,628 --> 00:12:45,929
same blob, listed again by the new tree.

170
00:12:46,269 --> 00:12:51,357
Two files look identical and have different
IDs. Root cause: they differ in bytes

171
00:12:51,397 --> 00:12:56,485
you cannot see, such as a carriage return;
cmp, od -c and git hash-object on both

172
00:12:56,525 --> 00:12:56,992
show it.

173
00:12:57,332 --> 00:13:02,565
Quoting an abbreviated ID in a report or
a script. Root cause: an abbreviation is

174
00:13:02,605 --> 00:13:07,383
unique only in one repository at one
time, and can become ambiguous as the

175
00:13:07,423 --> 00:13:09,857
repository grows; write all 40 digits.

176
00:13:10,197 --> 00:13:14,686
Expecting hand-typed commits to have the
book's commit IDs when the files are

177
00:13:14,726 --> 00:13:19,568
identical. Root cause: the same files
committed one second later give the same tree

178
00:13:19,608 --> 00:13:23,038
ID and a different commit ID,
because the times are hashed.

179
00:13:24,878 --> 00:13:30,467
Now, out of the lab. A backend team rolls
back a bad release, and someone asks: "Are

180
00:13:30,507 --> 00:13:33,885
we sure every file is back
to the last good state?"

181
00:13:33,925 --> 00:13:39,313
Nobody compares files. After the rollback,
git rev-parse 'HEAD^{tree}' prints the

182
00:13:39,353 --> 00:13:44,875
tree ID of the last good commit, and that
proves that every tracked file is back in

183
00:13:44,915 --> 00:13:49,968
that state. One ID, and the on-call
engineer gets their Friday evening back.

184
00:13:50,308 --> 00:13:55,431
The cost side, from the textbook. In a
repository of 40,000 files, an edit to two

185
00:13:55,471 --> 00:14:00,657
files in the ranker directory under services
writes two blobs, three trees, namely

186
00:14:00,697 --> 00:14:04,163
ranker, services and the top
directory, and one commit.

187
00:14:04,203 --> 00:14:09,262
A commit costs what changed, not what
exists. And any commit can be checked out,

188
00:14:09,302 --> 00:14:13,214
built or compared on its own. No
chain of patches is replayed.

189
00:14:15,054 --> 00:14:20,292
Your turn. Do Lab 1.2, "Snapshots
share unchanged blobs", in

190
00:14:20,332 --> 00:14:25,219
lab-manual/m01-what-git-is.md,
by hand in the lab shell.

191
00:14:25,559 --> 00:14:30,505
Before each commit in the lab, predict two
numbers: how many objects the commit will

192
00:14:30,545 --> 00:14:34,423
add, and how many of the blob IDs
in its tree you've already seen.

193
00:14:34,463 --> 00:14:36,322
Then list the objects and check.

194
00:14:38,162 --> 00:14:42,920
Q16: "A commit changes one file out
of 5,000, three directories deep.

195
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:46,605
How many objects does it
create, and of which types?"

196
00:14:46,945 --> 00:14:50,182
Answer out loud, and derive
the number. Don't recite it.

197
00:14:50,222 --> 00:14:54,921
A strong answer walks from the changed
file upward, says which objects have to be

198
00:14:54,961 --> 00:14:59,661
new and why, says which are reused, and
mentions the one object that every commit

199
00:14:59,701 --> 00:15:04,459
adds. And if you can also say what would
make your count wrong, you understand the

200
00:15:04,499 --> 00:15:06,214
rule and not only the example.

201
00:15:08,054 --> 00:15:13,282
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say: a commit records the whole project

202
00:15:13,322 --> 00:15:17,843
as the ID of one tree, with its
parents, author, committer and message.

203
00:15:17,883 --> 00:15:21,633
It contains no diff. git show
computes one from the parent.

204
00:15:21,673 --> 00:15:26,516
An unchanged file is the same blob,
referenced again, so a commit costs what

205
00:15:26,556 --> 00:15:27,029
changed.

206
00:15:27,369 --> 00:15:32,593
An object's ID is a hash of its type and
content, so the same content has the same

207
00:15:32,633 --> 00:15:37,665
ID in every repository, and one changed
byte changes the blob ID, every tree ID

208
00:15:37,705 --> 00:15:42,865
above it, and the commit ID. That gives
me deduplication, immutability, integrity

209
00:15:42,905 --> 00:15:44,213
and cheap comparison.

210
00:15:44,553 --> 00:15:49,488
And that disputed report? You can now
answer both of the CTO's questions, one

211
00:15:49,528 --> 00:15:50,392
sentence each.

